Z||000000||fierogt88||01-19-2001||01:48 PM||||Our dryer isn't making enough heat.  Is there normally some kind of heater element that can be easily repaired or is this a job for superman? er.. repairman?<P>TIA||||reg||8
Z||000001||Raydar||01-19-2001||01:57 PM||||Check your fuses. I've seen some dryers that used one leg of a 220 circuit for the motor, and the other leg for the heating element. If one fuse blew, the motor would run but the heater wouldn't come on. Also make sure the filter and ducts are clear. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fret Naught||01-21-2001||01:48 AM||||Easy fix most likely, it is a clogged up vent ie duct work. when it gets plugged or restricted, ther is a little klixon thermol switch that cuts out the element.  If that doesn't fix the problem, then pull the dryer away from the wall, take the back off this is where the element(s) are located. (I am assuming that you have an electrical dryer, and that you have enough common sense to unplug it before you proceed.) you will be able to tell right away if the element is bad, because it is a spring looking thing, that generally burns in two and dangels when it is bad. check the make and model number of your dryer and you will be able to pick up a new element at any appliance repair place. If it is putting out some heat but just not enough your problem is very likely a clogged vent. Good luck,||||reg||14
